WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.635
-Welcome back to "Late Night,"
everybody.

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:03.836
So, I don't know
if you know this,

00:00:03.836 --> 00:00:05.171
but, recently,
researchers in Australia

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:08.407
named a new species of insect
after RuPaul.

00:00:08.407 --> 00:00:10.643
It's called the Opaluma RuPaul.

00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:13.045 line:0%
Here it is.
It is colorful.

00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:15.948 line:0%
It is rainbow-hued.
It's just a good-looking bug.

00:00:15.948 --> 00:00:18.484 line:0%
And this is
really exciting news.

00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:20.086
I found out that
I am also going to

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:21.588
have a new bug named after me.

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:23.723
Here to introduce it is
a member of

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:25.924
the Entomological society
of America.

00:00:25.924 --> 00:00:27.060
Todd Conn, everybody.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.095
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:32.164 --> 00:00:34.000
-Thanks for having me, Seth.
-Of course.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.536
And, first of all, I just want
to say this is an honor.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:39.305
I am thrilled to have
an insect named after me.

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:42.108
-You know, like we saw with
the Opaluma RuPaul,

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:45.110
naming new species of insects
after celebrities

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.280
helps attract attention
to our conservation efforts.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.048
So, as an entomologist,

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:51.017
I'm very grateful to you
for participating.

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:52.685
-Well, you are very welcome.

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:54.687 line:0%
-And with that,
I would like to introduce

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:58.992 line:0%
our newly discovered specimen,
the Scarabaeus SethMeyers.

00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:06.832
-Uh...huh.

00:01:06.832 --> 00:01:08.668
-Pretty thrilling, right?

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:11.738
-That's the bug?
-That's your bug.

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:14.207
You know, our researchers
were beyond thrilled

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:16.075
to name this specimen
the SethMeyers

00:01:16.075 --> 00:01:19.178
and honor you and your legacy
in late-night comedy.

00:01:19.178 --> 00:01:22.115
-So, like, what --
Is it some kind of cockroach?

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:23.449
-Oh, no, no, no.

00:01:23.449 --> 00:01:26.853
The SethMeyers is technically
a variety of dung beetle.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:30.456
-A dung beetle.

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:32.592
-Yeah, you know what dung is,
don't you, Seth?

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:33.593
-Yeah, I know.

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:34.961
-It's [bleep]

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:37.897
We actually call it the
[bleep] bug around the office.

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:40.366
-Great.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:42.936
-So, you see, the SethMeyers
is the first variety

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:47.673
of dung beetle that consumes
its own feces for sustenance.

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:49.642
In fact, due to
a special digestive enzyme,

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:52.212
the SethMeyers beetle
survives almost exclusively

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:53.947
by devouring its own feces.

00:01:57.417 --> 00:02:01.754
-So, I guess I had
something else in mind.

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:02.988
-Well, you know, Seth, you don't
get to pick your insect.

00:02:02.988 --> 00:02:04.323
We do.
-Yeah.

00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:07.226
-Just, you know, the RuPaul bug,
you know, was colorful.

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:08.794
It was just more exciting.

00:02:08.794 --> 00:02:11.030
-Oh, trust me, the SethMeyers
is very exciting.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.264
-It is?

00:02:12.264 --> 00:02:14.767
-You know, for example,
it constantly secretes

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:19.973
this runny, snot-like mucus,
which is pretty fascinating.

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:21.774
Plus, the SethMeyers
is famous for emitting

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:24.109
this noxious, rancid odor

00:02:24.109 --> 00:02:26.946
that has never before been
observed in nature.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:28.748
Do you have any idea
why that might be, Seth?

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:32.084
-Is it because it eats
its own feces?

00:02:32.084 --> 00:02:34.053
-Hey! That's right.

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:36.389
You know, you may have
a future in entomology.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.224
-Would I get to
pick my own insect then?

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:39.592
-No.

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:41.828
Now, one thing
I did want to add --

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:43.396
in order to thank you
for your help,

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:45.064
we've put you in
the latest issue of

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:46.532
"The American Entomologist,"

00:02:46.532 --> 00:02:48.001
where we're featuring
celebrity insects.

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.268
So, here you go.

00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:51.838
There's Daniel Craig and
his blue-eyed damselfly.

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:54.406 line:0%
And there's you with
your dung beetle

00:02:54.406 --> 00:02:57.677 line:0%
getting ready to eat
a big bowl of feces.

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.412
-So, Daniel Craig's bug
has blue eyes.

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:00.913
It's very obvious
how that connects to him.

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:03.582
You know, I also have blue eyes.
It doesn't matter.

00:03:03.582 --> 00:03:04.750
I guess I just
don't really see how

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.452
the dung beetle connects to me.

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:09.455
-You know how you do your talk
show at 12:30 a.m. every night?

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:10.523
-Uh-huh.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.325
-Well, that happens to be
the exact time

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:14.426
the SethMeyers beetle
starts its day.

00:03:14.426 --> 00:03:16.562
and is typically eating
its first meal,

00:03:16.562 --> 00:03:17.563
which is, of course...

00:03:17.563 --> 00:03:20.800
-Feces.
-Feces, yeah.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.068
-Well, I'm happy to help
with a good cause,

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.368
and I guess I'm honored to
have this dung beetle

00:03:23.368 --> 00:03:24.370
named after me.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.604
-That's great.

00:03:25.604 --> 00:03:27.740
You know, ultimately,
I hope things like this

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.710
help people see that insects --
they're living creatures,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.979
like you and me,
and by connecting to them,

00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:34.647
we're connecting to
a larger ecosystem

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:35.982
that holds us all together.

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:38.151
-You know,
that was beautifully put.

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:39.452
-Oh, my God!

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:41.020
[Bleep]
Did you see that?!

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.889
Oh, my good Lord!

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:45.858
That was one of the biggest
SethMeyers I've ever seen.

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.660
One of them
must have gotten out of the lab.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.395
Oh, look at this [bleep]

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:52.131
There's SethMeyers goo
all over me.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:53.399
Oh, my God!

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.601
You know, we might want to
clear out the studio,

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:57.336
because this place is about to
stink like SethMeyers.

00:03:57.336 --> 00:03:59.272
-Alright, you know what?
We'll be right back.

